\section{The density}{

For \code{W} the summed Hermitian matrix at one frequency and \code{n} its
degrees of freedom,

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{log f(W) = (n - 2) log det W - tr(S^-1 W) - n log det S - log CGamma_2(n)
log CGamma_2(n) = log(pi) + lgamma(n) + lgamma(n - 1)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

In this package's coordinates both hard parts collapse. Writing
\code{S11}, \code{S22} for the powers, \code{C} for the coherence and \code{phi} for the
phase, \verb{det S = S11 S22 (1 - C)}, so the log determinant is a sum of
three logs and no determinant is taken; and \verb{tr(S^-1 W)} is

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{(S22 w11 + S11 w22 - 2 sqrt(C S11 S22) (cos(phi) w12r + sin(phi) w12i))
  / (S11 S22 (1 - C))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

which is real arithmetic throughout. \strong{At two channels the complex
Wishart needs no complex arithmetic on the tape.} RTMB 1.9's
\code{adcomplex} was used to derive and check this form at general \code{p},
and it works, including under a Laplace approximation; but at \code{p = 2}
it was measured 5.8 times slower than the closed form for the same
answer to six decimals, so the closed form is what ships.
