\section{The trap}{
 a power formula too simple for the data:
The likelihood couples the powers and the coherence. A power model too
simple for the spectrum does not merely leave \code{mu} and \code{pow2} wrong:
it reweights the rows each coherence parameter pools, and moves the
coherence with them. It converges without complaint.

\strong{Check it by comparing AIC with and without a frequency term on the
powers.} Nothing here does it for you. A draft of this package
warned automatically from a \code{fit_check} hook that measured the spread
of the power residuals, and that statistic is not monotone in the
coherence displacement it was meant to catch: it went silent at a
2.7-fold power spread where the reported coherence was already nearly
double the truth, and it fired at a ratio of 20.5 on a correctly
specified model whose coherences were right. It was withdrawn rather
than retuned, because a check that punishes correct models teaches
users to ignore it.

An AIC comparison does work, and works where the hook did not. Two
bands over 400 frequencies at \code{n = 8}, true coherences 0.35 and 0.05,
\code{coh ~ band}, varying the power spread across the record:\tabular{llll}{
   power spread \tab flat powers report \tab correct powers report \tab delta AIC \cr
   1.6x \tab 0.295, 0.083 \tab 0.330, 0.068 \tab 80 \cr
   2.7x \tab 0.268, 0.078 \tab 0.342, 0.041 \tab 487 \cr
   4.4x \tab 0.256, 0.136 \tab 0.344, 0.054 \tab 954 \cr
   7.3x \tab 0.240, 0.206 \tab 0.342, 0.047 \tab 1849 \cr
   19.6x \tab 0.229, 0.458 \tab 0.341, 0.052 \tab 3716 \cr
   385x \tab 0.222, 0.875 \tab 0.342, 0.059 \tab 11518 \cr
}


The flat fit reports the high band as nine times its true coherence
at a 19.6-fold spread and as seventeen times at 385-fold, and AIC
rejects it by 80 or more even at the mildest spread tested. The
correctly specified fits recover 0.35 and 0.05 throughout.

So: fit the powers twice, once with a frequency term and once
without, and read the coherence off the one AIC prefers.
\strong{Model the powers before reading any coherence.}
}

\section{Why the links are the constraint, in floating point too}{

The spectral matrix must be positive definite at every frequency, in
every group, for every draw the optimizer tries. \verb{det S > 0} needs
only \code{C < 1}, and \code{C} reaches the density through a logit link, so no
value of any linear predictor can produce an invalid matrix \strong{in
exact arithmetic}. No eigenvalue floor and no projection step are
applied, and none is needed.

Exact arithmetic is not what runs. \code{plogis(eta)} is exactly 1 in
double precision from about \code{eta = 36.74} upward, so a density that
formed \code{1 - C} by subtraction would read \code{log(0)} and divide by zero
there, and would already be wrong in the third digit at \code{eta = 30}.
That region is reachable: a random effect on \code{coh} with one group
near a true coherence of 1 drives the estimate past \code{eta = 34}.

This family therefore never subtracts. Core stores each dpar's linear
predictor beside it, and the logit link's \code{logit_eta} field makes the
log-odds exact, so \code{log(1 - C)} is computed as
\code{-logspace_add(0, eta)} and \code{1 / (1 - C)} as its exponential. Both
are exact to \code{eta = 709}, where the double range itself ends, against
\code{36.74} for the naive form. Measured against a high-precision
reference at \code{n = 16}: relative error 1.0e-03 at \code{eta = 30} and NaN
at \code{eta = 40} before the change, below 1e-15 at both after it.

One path keeps the plain round trip, and says so rather than being
floored: \code{residuals(type = "deviance")} runs off the tape, where core
does not store the linear predictor, so it uses \code{log1p(-C)}. That is
accurate until \code{C} rounds to exactly 1, which needs \code{eta} past 36.74
and is further than a fit reaches in practice. Measured on the
degenerate case above, two signals differing by 1e-5 of noise: the
fit lands at \code{eta = 23.0}, \code{C} is 0.99999999989743915 rather than 1,
and all 127 response, Pearson and deviance residuals are finite.
Past 36.74 they would be \code{NaN}, and no floor is applied to hide it.
}

\section{What it refuses}{

\itemize{
\item More than two channels. This is a scope decision, and \strong{core is not
the obstacle}: \code{frm()} carries a matrix-valued response, and both
a gaussian fit and a custom family indexing \code{y[, 1]} and \code{y[, 2]}
work today. What is missing is here rather than there. A
\code{p}-channel model needs \code{p^2} linear predictors written out by
hand; the clean \verb{power, power, coherence, phase} coordinates of
this family do not survive past \code{p = 2}, so it would be written on
raw Cholesky entries that mean nothing to a reader; and the log
determinant and the trace stop being closed forms, which puts
\code{adcomplex} back on the tape at about six times the cost. The
density itself generalizes and was checked at \code{p = 4}. Fit pairs.
\item \code{n < 2}. A single complex draw gives a rank-one matrix whose
coherence is exactly 1 and whose log determinant is \code{-Inf}; the
normalizing constant is \code{Inf} there as well, since
\code{lgamma(n - 1)} reaches a non-positive argument. This is checked
in \code{valid_y()}, before any tape is built.
\item \code{simulate()}. A draw from this family is a whole Hermitian matrix
and the response slot carries one number of the four, so a
simulated \code{w11} with the observed \code{w22}, \code{w12r} and \code{w12i} beside
it is not a draw from anything. \code{\link[=frm_cross_simulate]{frm_cross_simulate()}} returns all
four columns instead.
}
}

